y blatantly shill for their  government as a PR arm for the Kremlin  numerous RT reporters have angrily  resigned from the network saying that  they were tired of doing Putin's bidding  RT has reported on the Russia dossier as  though there's no way that it's  authentic they've inferred time and  again that there was positively no  collusion between Russia and the Trump  campaign they've reported on Trump's  claims about the Obama wiretapping  without properly expressing how truly  unfounded the claims are when news  breaks about Trump team members having  met with Russian officials RT frames it  as America merely trying to repair  relations with Moscow downplaying the  aspects about people like Michael Flynn  and Jeff Sessions having lied about the  communication RT is more of a government  propaganda outlet than fake news per se  most of the time a site like Breitbart  shouldn't really be considered fake news  either because it is technically usually  reporting real information and  manipulating the narrative or  selectively presenting facts in order to  send a certain message there are  exceptions we all know that Breitbart  will report falsehoods from time to time  but I think that in theory it's fair  say that it's propagandistic opinion not  actual fake news an opinion isn't even  really news anyway conspiracy websites  like Infowars aren't really fake news  either those are kind of in their own  strange category many of Infowars  stories do contain real information but  they often go off the deep end with  speculation and connecting dots in a way  that makes no sense  granted it's sometimes hard to tell  whether Alex Jones and Infowars even  really believe the stuff they're putting  out there and it does border on fake  news but in principle I don't think it  serves any purpose to label them as such  the fake news moniker should be saved  for stories and media outlets whose sole  mission is to mislead people on purpose  and it's important to single out and  denounce actual fake news that fits the  proper MO that's why it's so  counterproductive the way that Donald  Trump and people in his camp go around  calling everything fake news anything  that doesn't paint a positive picture of  Trump is fake news to him that's why the  term has lost so much of its meaning I'm  not going to give you a message and you  stay counter you are fake dude circa and  you say categorically that nobody know  miss president like that really just  watched the totally biased and fake news  reports of the so called Russia story on  NBC and ABC such dishonesty Russia talk  is fake news put out by the Dems and  plate up by the media in order to mask  the big election defeat and the illegal  leaks the fake news media failing you  know times  NBC News ABC CBS CNN is not my enemy it  is the enemy of the American people  throwing around the term fake news like  this is just ignorant I think Trump is  just trying to get people to distrust  the media so that people won't believe  the media when they report bad things  about him if it finally gets printed  that there's solid evidence of collusion  between Trump and Russia during the 2016  election or that we finally have  evidence that Trump isn't as rich as he  says he is for example by that point  Trump wants to have already discredited  news outlets preemptively so many times  that his supporters won't believe  anything that's reported the Nazis used  a sin  tactic during world war ii they use the  term lugan press meaning lying press to  refer to any media in germany or  elsewhere that spoke negatively about  the Nazi Party Trump supporters at a  rally in October of 2016 were actually  caught on camera yelling lugan press at  journalists  [Applause]  right government intervention can be a  way to fight back against fake news to  an extent in some situations there was  language written into the 2017 National  Defense Authorization Act that asks  federal agencies to combat and publicize  foreign propaganda and disinformation  operations and I see that as a good  thing but when it comes to the idea of  government actually censoring or  filtering speech and journalism that may  well become a slippery slope a road not  worth going down the best defense  against fake news is simple media  literacy knowing how to identify fake  news and propaganda news media literacy  is a major problem in the United States  given how easily fake news is able to  thrive online  ask yourself certain questions when you  come across a news article online was  this article posted on social media by  someone you know who posts many  untrustworthy articles many of us have  friends like that on Facebook does the  website have an odd name pay attention  to the URL does it end with something  weird like dot-com CEO is it an obscure  foreign URL this can also sometimes be a  red flag does the site have very generic  click Beatty advertisements that's also  rarely a good sign look at the websites  other articles to see if the website as  a whole  appears untrustworthy do background  research on the site read the about Us  section of the site is there even such a  section of the site you can also google  the website name and fact check or  source watch after the websites name our  other reputable news sites reporting on  the same story read the comments section  are people calling it out as fake news  look into the author of the article is  there even an author listed what are the  sources do the sources seem legitimate  are there any sources does the  information and evidence in the article  support the claim that is made in the  headline does the story attack a generic  enemy like the media Trump supporters  the left pay attention to the quote  in the story and who said them are there  quotes  who said the quotes are they in context  does all of the information rely on one  groundbreaking or questionable factoid  how much evidence is actually there does  the article engage with anyone who  disagrees  are there many spelling or grammar  errors this can be a sign that the site  has no editors which can also help to  inform us about the website's  credibility not all of these are  foolproof ways of determining whether  something is fake news or not but it  does help to have these questions in  mind as a reader the same if so  public-affairs poll I cited before found  that people who primarily get their news  from Facebook are much less likely to  even be able to recognize fake news and  one in three Americans says that they  see fake news in their social media  feeds on a regular basis we may not  realize it but it is pervasive get your  news from a variety of sources question  and challenge people who post fake news  on social media they may not be aware of  what they're doing and they might  actually appreciate you helping them not  look so gullible in the future maybe do  it in a private message so you're not  embarrassing them publicly in the case  of pizza gate and the leaked John  Podesta emails about comet ping pong in  Washington DC fake news actually caused  someone to show up at a restaurant with  a gun and start shooting this stuff has  real-life consequences but one of the  worst consequences is that everyday  people are being duped easily and many  of those people vote we're only in the  beginning stages of finding out to what  degree Russia interferes with our  politics and in what ways if we ever  find out at all but the fight against  fake news disinformation and propaganda  starts with being able to identify it  and you have to be able to not go around  labeling anything you disagree with as  fake news the way our president does the  boy who cried fake news and we must  actively combat it by adopting and  promoting good habits as consumers of  media understanding how journey  works and understanding how deception  works those might be the most crucial  components of being a functional member  of a democracy
 

